u = N$ is the degree of freedom, $j_{	ext{Model}}^{	ext{LIS}}(E_k)$ represents the interstellar particle flux given by the model at the kinetic energy of $E_k$ with an associated error of $\sigma_{k, 	ext{Model}}^2$, and there are $N$ energy bins in total. <!-- page 16 -->
Here, we show the LIS for 26 species of GCR nuclei in Figures 6 and 7, and calculate $\chi_{
u}^2$ for each particle type in different energy ranges including the low, high, as well as overall ranges as presented in Table 4. Specifically, we show the comparisons of the LIS of protons and helium ions employed in SDEMMA and measured by Voyager 1 during 2012/342–2015/181 in Cummings et al. (2016) in Figures 6(b) and (d). Considering that the modulation is relatively weak for particles with rigidities above 100 GV (Corti et al. 2016, 2019), here, we also include AMS-02 measurements from 2011 May to 2018 May above 100 GV for LIS comparisons in Figure 6 (Aguilar et al. 2021b). Remarkably, the relative difference consistently falls within a narrow margin of ~15% for both low- and high-energy parts, and the most accurate reproduction in models, i.e., 0% difference, is mostly encompassed by the error bars. The data in Table 4 highlight that the LIS among models is considerably different from one another, particularly at low energies for all particle types. In contrast, the differences persist at high energies but appear notably smaller for most species. Figures 6 and 7 offer a detailed comparison of the LIS, revealing that the LIS can differ with 2 to 3 orders of magnitude at very low energies ($E \lesssim 10^2$ MeV nuc$^{-1}$). To amplify the differences at high energies, we employ a scaling factor $E^{2.7}$ to enhance visibility, but still, the LIS of many elements exhibits a good agreement in magnitude among these models. However, despite the differences in the LIS taken as the boundary condition and model complexities, the predicted results given by the models are remarkably similar to one another, as illustrated in Figure 3. The contrast of models perhaps indicates that the underlying physics governing the transport and modulation of GCRs are perhaps different among these models. It also implies that differences in the LIS below ~100 MeV nuc$^{-1}$ have little impact on the GCR spectra near Earth, because the particles in this energy range may have lost most of their energy during the way to 1 au, especially under the process of adiabatic cooling (e.g., see Section 7 of Moraal 2013; Gieseler et al. 2017; and Engelbrecht et al. 2022). Furthermore, these low-energy particles contribute very little to the actual risk for humans and instruments in space behind shielding (e.g., see Durante & Cucinotta 2011; Mrigakshi et al. 2013; Slaba & Blattnig 2014, and the references therein). Ultimately, when compared with the state-of-the-art particle or dose measurements in the heliosphere, different models can fit their parameters and setups so long as they are physically reasonable. The adaptability of these models can explain their similar performance in predicting the deep space radiation environment at Earth as shown in Section 3.1, even with apparent differences in LIS and different complexities in models. As for the discrepancies, the BON20 and HelMod LIS functions have been rigorously validated against the PAMELA and AMS-02 data with a high rigidity ($R > 1$ GV) by running the corresponding models, the latter of which has been calibrated by the Voyager 1 observations at rigidities less than 1 GV (e.g., Cummings et al. 2016; Boschini et al. 2017, 2020, 2021). Based on the work of Nymmik et al. (1992, 1996), the ISO 15390 model also takes their LIS into use, which are validated against the experimental observations during the 1960s–1990s concentrating on energies lower than $10^3$ MeV nuc$^{-1}$, but with errors of ~20% for energies above 30 MeV nuc$^{-1}$, while the errors for low energies are several times higher. As a result, the high-energy part may show relatively good agreements, whereas the flux at low energies diverges, probably ascribable to the detector resolutions and experimental conditions. ## 4.2. Proton, Helium, and Iron Flux As illustrated in Figure 5, protons and helium ions stand out as the most significant contributors to the absorbed dose, followed by iron nuclei among the heavy ions. For protons and helium ions, the model–observation comparisons are presented in Figures 8 and 9, respectively. The data spans from 2006 July to 2011 April for PAMELA and from 2011 May to 2019 October for AMS-02 without overlaps. Overall, the discrepancies between models and observations are minor: $|RD| \lesssim 10\%$ and $\chi_{
u}^2 \lesssim 10$, probably on account of the focus on these two particles for their dominance in abundance. 1. During the SC 23/24 minimum, as shown in Figure 8(a), all models except CRÈME basically fall within the error bars for proton flux, consistent with the low values of $|RD|$ and $\chi_{
u}^2$ in Figures 8(d) and (e). On the contrary, as shown in Figure 9(a), most of the models appear to overestimate the helium ion flux to different degrees, corresponding to the large values of $|RD|$ and $\chi_{
u}^2$ in Figures 9(d) and (e). 2. At the SC 24 maximum (see Figure 8(b)), HelMod, SDEMMA, and BON20 seem to be more competitive for proton flux predictions, while the results of CRÈME and DLR highly deviate from the observational data. As for helium ions, all the models basically align well with the AMS-02 data at this epoch. 3. Near the SC 24/25 minimum, for the observed energy range, only HelMod provides a perfect prediction of the H flux as shown in Figure 8(c), while all models except SDEMMA give consistent He flux in the energy range for validation as depicted in Figure 9(c). Despite the varying degrees of disagreements among the models and the observational data, especially in the most crucial and sensitive energy ranges for radiation protection (on the order of $10^2	ext{--}10^5$ MeV nuc$^{-1}$, see Section 4.1 in Liu et al. 2023), the deviations of the model-predicted absorbed dose and the actual measurements still remain less than $\lesssim 20\%$ for most of the time (see Figure 3(c)). As for Fe, the model outputs are compared with the ACE/CRIS data at low energies and 8.5 yr averaged observations from AMS-02 at high energies. Figure 10(d) indicates that most of the models nicely predict the iron flux at high energies, except for BON20 with apparent underestimations. At low energies, as shown in Figures 10(e) and (f), the values of $|RD|$ and $\chi_{
u}^2$ fluctuate over time and are notably higher in 2014 and 2022 with $|RD| \gtrsim 30\%$, twice as much as usual, for all models except BON20, likely due to its validation against ACE/CRIS and limited balloon measurements (see the right panel of Figure 9; and related texts in Slaba & Whitman 2020). Comparisons in 2014 February, shown in Figure 10(b), highlight obvious discrepancies for SDEMMA and HelMod. At this epoch (near the SC 24 maximum), the measured particle spectrum has a flattening shape within the detected energy region, while the model-predicted ones have a curved shape with a peak based on the embedded setups. <!-- page 17 -->
