# A DRIFT MODEL FOR THE MODULATION OF GALACTIC COSMIC RAYS M. S. POTGIETER AND H. MORAAL Department of Physics, Potchefstroom University for C.H.E., Potchefstroom, South Africa Received 1984 October 16; accepted 1985 January 29 # ABSTRACT Detailed numerical solutions of the cosmic-ray transport equation, including drift effects in the interplanetary magnetic field, are presented. This is part of the effort to understand the role and importance of these effects. Jokipii and co-workers have published a series of drift models in this Journal. Their models range from the relatively simple to a full three-dimensional one. Nevertheless, some confusion still exists about the real significance of drift effects. This paper therefore has essentially two purposes. First, our independently developed model generally confirms the results of Jokipii et al. This largely resolves previous questions about discrepancies between independent model predictions. Second, we stress the point that the drift effect and its importance can only be judged by simultaneous simulations of all available modulation features. This restriction has not previously been applied to drift models, and it is shown that it considerably narrows the possible parameter space for modulation conditions. The model successfully simulates proton and electron spectra together with high-energy anisotropies in consecutive solar cycles, using one single set of modulation parameters. Since conventional models experience difficulties in simulating these observations, we conclude that drift should seriously be considered to be related to all modulation phenomena which exhibit a 22 yr cycle. Subject headings: cosmic rays: general — interplanetary medium # I. INTRODUCTION The past eight years have seen a continuing high level of effort to understand the role of drift in cosmic-ray modulation. (See reviews by Jones 1983; Quenby 1984). Jokipii, Levy, and Hubbard (1977) were the first to point out the relative importance of drift effects in the dipolar background interplanetary magnetic field (IMF), which switches its dipole moment approximately every 11 yr. The first numerical models which included drift were reported by Jokipii and Kopriva (1979a, b) and Moraal, Gleeson, and Webb (1979). Both groups concluded that the inclusion of drift in the modulation process produces novel and fundamentally important effects. The predictions of these initial models were, however, contradictory. The model of Moraal, Gleeson, and Webb did not treat the drift in the equatorial neutral sheet properly, while Jokipii and Davila (1981) augmented their earlier results with more realistic diffusion coefficients. Jokipii and Thomas (1981) subsequently incorporated the effect of a wavy neutral sheet into their drift model. Recently, Kóta and Jokipii (1983) developed a full three-dimensional drift model. The revised and extended results of this group have not yet been independently confirmed. In addition, Jokipii and co-workers concentrated mainly on the proton modulation to establish the general effects of drift. The full impact of drift on various other modulation features such as electron spectra, density gradients, anisotropies, and neutron-monitor observations have, however, not yet been established in detail. In this paper we therefore (a) illustrate the general characteristics of the solutions of the steady-state transport equation including drift, (b) show the influence of various parameter variations on these solutions, (c) establish to what extent our model predictions agree with those of Jokipii and Davila (1981), and (d) illustrate that the model can simultaneously, that is, with one single set of parameters, explain modulation features which seem to be related to the reversal of the solar magnetic field. # II. THE DRIFT MODEL Inside the heliosphere the cosmic-ray differential number density $U_{p}(\boldsymbol{r}, p, t)$ with respect to momentum p, position r, and time t, is governed by the equation of transport: $$
\frac {\partial U _ {p}}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot (V U _ {p} - \mathbf {K} \cdot \nabla U _ {p}) - \frac {1}{3} (\nabla \cdot V) \frac {\partial}{\partial p} (p U _ {p}) = 0, \tag {1}
$$ or, alternatively (see Jokipii, Levy, and Hubbard 1977), $$
\frac {\partial U _ {p}}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot [ (V + \langle \boldsymbol {v} _ {d} \rangle) U _ {p} - \mathbf {K} ^ {(s)} \cdot \nabla U _ {p} ]
$$ $$
- \frac {1}{3} (\nabla \cdot V) \frac {\partial}{\partial p} (p U _ {p}) = 0. \tag {2}
$$ Here V is the solar wind velocity and $$
\mathbf {K} (\boldsymbol {r}, p, t) = \left| \begin{array}{c c c} K _ {\parallel} & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & K _ {\perp} & K _ {T} \\ 0 & - K _ {T} & K _ {\perp} \end{array} \right| \tag {3}
$$ is the diffusion tensor of the cosmic ray particles in the IMF. The symmetric part of the tensor (3) is denoted by $\mathbf{K}^{(s)}$ . The pitch-angle averaged drift velocity over the near-isotropic particle distribution is given by $$
\langle \boldsymbol {v} _ {d} \rangle = \boldsymbol {\nabla} \times K _ {T} \boldsymbol {e} _ {B}, \tag {4}
$$ with $e_{B}$ the unit vector in the direction of the IMF, denoted by B. The streaming vector or differential current density is given by $$
\boldsymbol {S} _ {p} = C V U _ {p} - \mathbf {K} \cdot \nabla U _ {p}, \tag {5}
$$ or $$
\boldsymbol {S} _ {p} = C V U _ {p} - \mathbf {K} ^ {(s)} \cdot \nabla U _ {p} - K _ {T} \boldsymbol {e} _ {B} \times \nabla U _ {p}. \tag {6}
$$ Here C is the Compton-Getting factor and the term $CVU_{p}$ implicitly includes the electric-field drift velocity $E \times B/B^{2}$ , as shown by Forman and Gleeson (1975). In order to illustrate the relative importance of drift effects on the modulation of cosmic rays, it is necessary to choose all the parameters in equations (1)-(4) as realistic as possible. We therefore discuss them in turn. The radial solar-wind speed used in this model was chosen as a rapidly rising function of radial distance from the surface of the Sun at $r = r_{\odot}$ to a near constant value: $$
V (r) = 4 0 0 [ 1 - \exp {(1 3. 3 (r _ {\odot} - r))} ] \mathrm {km s^ {- 1}}, \tag {7}
$$ where $r$ and $r_{\odot}=0.005$ are expressed in astronomical units (AU). This form is in accord with recent measurements (e.g., Collard, Milhalov, and Wolfe 1982). The standard spiral IMF configuration used in the model is given by $$
\boldsymbol {B} = \frac {B _ {0}}{r ^ {2}} \left(\boldsymbol {e} _ {r} - \tan \psi \boldsymbol {e} _ {\phi}\right), \tag {8}
$$ where $\psi$ is the angle between the direction of the field lines and the radial direction, measured in a heliocentric spherical coordinate system $(r, \theta, \phi)$ . If $\Omega$ is the angular velocity of the Sun about its axis, then $\tan \psi = \Omega(r - r_{\odot}) \sin \theta / V$ . Historically, the most important feature of the IMF concerning the modulation of galactic cosmic rays is not the underlying spiral pattern but rather its small-scale irregularities which scatter cosmic rays in pitch angle. Diffusion theories have not yet been successful to predict the coefficients $K_{\parallel}, K_{\perp}$ , and $K_{T}$ for any general level of turbulence of the IMF (see, e.g., the review by Quenby 1984). Therefore the coefficient $K_{\parallel}$ was chosen by taking into account the following observations and considerations. First, Palmer (1982) summarized all analyses of intensity-time profiles of solar cosmic-ray events to conclude that at Earth the parallel mean free path $\lambda_{\parallel} = 3K_{\parallel}/v$ has a weak rigidity ( $P$ ) dependence below $P \approx 3$ GV and lies in the range $0.08 \lesssim \lambda_{\parallel} \lesssim 0.3$ AU. Second, quasi-linear diffusion theory (QLT) and observations seem to agree that $K_{\perp}/K_{\parallel} \ll 1$ , and therefore the effective radial diffusion coefficient $K_{rr} = K_{\parallel} \cos^2 \psi + K_{\perp} \sin^2 \psi$ may be approximated as $K_{rr} \approx K_{\parallel} \cos^2 \psi$ . Morfill and Völk (1979) and Palmer (1982) agree that near the equatorial plane ( $\theta = \pi/2$ ), $K_{rr}$ is at most very weakly dependent on radial distance $r$ . Third, Morfill and Völk (1979) suggested quite a strong increase of $K_{rr}$ toward the polar regions. Finally, QLT predicts that the rigidity dependence of $K_{\parallel}$ at $P \gtrsim 1$ GV must be of the form $P^a$ with $1 \leq a \leq 2$ , while for $P \lesssim 1$ GV, observations suggest that $a \approx 0$ . Taking into account these observations and predictions, the most acceptable form for $K_{\parallel}$ proved to be $$
K _ {\parallel} = (K _ {\parallel}) _ {0} \left[ 1 + \left(\frac {r}{r _ {e}}\right) ^ {2} \right] \beta K _ {2} (P), \tag {9}
$$ with $\beta = v/c,\quad r_{e} = 1\quad AU,\quad 0.6 \times 10^{22} \leq (K_{\parallel})_{0} \leq 2.25 \times 10^{22}$ $cm^{2}s^{-1}, and$

