leration of cosmic rays in the solar wind. Webb (1975) however, took exception to this view. He argued that adiabatic deceleration can only be applicable in the case of convective transport, that is strong scattering where the components of the diffusion tensor ~0. The cosmic rays are then effectively constrained to move with the solar wind as they scatter between the IMF irregularities which behave like the walls of a 'magnetic box'. Consequently the particles change momentum at the adiabatic rate (3.7) within the magnetic box whose walls move with the solar wind velocity V. Webb then took a different point of view, and showed that cosmic rays with momentum p' relative to the solar wind lose momentum at a rate $$\langle \dot{p}' \rangle / p' = -\frac{1}{3} \nabla \cdot \mathbf{V}$$
 (3.8) because the solar wind reference frame is a non-inertial frame. This rate is identical to the adiabatic rate (3.7), and when transformed to the stationary frame, it results in (3.5). Therefore the difference in interpretation of cosmic-ray momentum changes by Jokipii and Parker on the one hand and Webb on the other hand does not effect the resulting transport equation. Alternative derivations of the transport equation were given by Gleeson and Axford (1967) and Dolginov and Toptygin (1967, 1968). These authors derived (3.6) from the Boltzmann equation in which the cosmic rays are scattered by hard sphere scattering centers embedded in the solar wind. In neither of these approaches adiabatic (or another form of) deceleration was postulated a priori. ## 3.2. SOLUTIONS OF THE MODULATION EQUATIONS To facilitate solutions and interpretations of (3.2) and (3.6) we assume a right-handed heliospherieal polar coordinate system (r, 0, qS) with unit vectors (er, e0, %) pointing radially outward, from the solar North Pole and in the azimuthal directions (east-west) respectively. If ~ is the angle between the radial and average magnetic field directions, the diffusion tensor (1.1), with to(, 1) = ~c~ 2) becomes $$\begin{cases}
\kappa_{rr} & \kappa_{r\theta} & \kappa_{r\phi} \\
\kappa_{\theta r} & \kappa_{\theta \theta} & \kappa_{\theta \phi}
\end{cases} = \begin{cases}
\kappa_{\parallel} \cos^{2} \psi + \kappa_{\perp} \sin^{2} \psi & -\kappa_{T} \sin \psi & (\kappa_{\perp} - \kappa_{\parallel}) \sin \psi \cos \psi \\
