We account for this by adopting the limiter suggested by Enßlin et al. (2007), and define as final acceleration efficiency $$
\zeta_ {\mathrm{inj}} = \left[ 1 - \exp \left(- \frac {\zeta_ {\mathrm{lin}}}{\zeta_ {\mathrm{max}}}\right) \right] \zeta_ {\mathrm{max}}. \tag {28}
$$ We will adopt $\zeta_{max} = 0.5$ for the results of this study. Thus, we take the injected energy to be $$
\Delta \tilde {\varepsilon} _ {\mathrm{inj}} = \zeta_ {\mathrm{inj}} \Delta u _ {\mathrm{diss}}, \tag {29}
$$ and correspondingly, the injected particle number is given by $$
\Delta \tilde {n} _ {\mathrm{inj}} = \frac {m _ {\mathrm{p}} \Delta \tilde {\varepsilon} _ {\mathrm{inj}}}{T _ {\mathrm{CR}} (\alpha_ {\mathrm{inj}} , q _ {0})}, \tag {30}
$$ where $T_{\mathrm{CR}}(\alpha_{\mathrm{inj}}, q_{0})$ is the mean kinetic energy of the accelerated cosmic ray particles. In practice, both $\Delta\tilde{\varepsilon}_{inj}$ and $\Delta\tilde{n}_{inj}$ will be lowered when we shift the actual injection point from $q_{0}$ to $q_{inj}$ , as determined by Eq. (18), with the difference of the energies fed to the thermal reservoir directly. It is clear that the efficiency of CR particle acceleration depends strongly on the compression ratio, or equivalently on the Mach number of shocks. Interestingly, accretion shocks during cosmological structure formation can be particularly strong. Here we hence expect potentially interesting effects both for the forming intragroup and intracluster media, as well as for the intergalactic medium. However, in order to accurately account for the cosmic ray injection by structure formation shocks, we somehow need to be able to estimate the strength of shocks in SPH simulations. As SPH captures shocks with an artificial viscosity instead of an explicit shock detection scheme, this is a non-trivial problem. In Pfrommer et al. (2006), our second companion paper to this study, we have proposed a practical solution to this problem and developed a novel method for measuring the Mach number of shocks on the fly during cosmological SPH calculation. The method relies on the entropy formulation for SPH by Springel & Hernquist (2002), and uses the current rate of entropy injection due to viscosity, together with an approximation for the numerical shock transit time, to estimate the shock Mach number. The scheme works better than one may have expected, and it is in fact capable of producing quite accurate Mach number estimates, even for the case of composite gases with a thermal and a cosmic ray pressure component. In cosmological simulations, the method delivers Mach number statistics which agree well with results obtained with hydrodynamical mesh codes that use explicit Riemann solvers (Ryu et al. 2003; Pfrommer et al. 2006). Having a reliable Mach number estimator solves an important problem when trying to account for cosmic ray injection by shocks in SPH. Another complication is posed by the shock broadening inherent in SPH, which implies a finite shock transit time for particles, i.e. a SPH particle may require several timesteps before it has passed through a shock and received the full dissipative heating. Note that the number of these steps depends on the timestep criterion employed, and can in principle be made very large for a sufficiently conservative choice of the Courant coefficient. Unlike assumed in the above treatment of diffusive shock acceleration, we hence are not dealing with a discrete injection event, but rather need to formulate the cosmic ray acceleration in a “continuous fashion”, in parallel to the thermal dissipation, such that the final result does not depend on how many timesteps are taken to resolve a broadened shock front. Fortunately, the above treatment can be easily adjusted to these conditions. We can simply insert for $\Delta u_{diss}$ in Eq. (29) the dissipated energy in the current timestep. This quantity is computed in the SPH formalism anyway, and in fact, we know that SPH will integrate $\Delta u_{diss}$ correctly through the shock profile, independent of the number of steps taken. This is because the correct pre- and post-shock state of the gas are enforced by the conservation laws, which are fulfilled by the conservative SPH code. For the same reason, the Rankine-Hugoniot jump conditions are reproduced across the broadened shock. Note however that for computing the linear shock injection efficiency according to Eq. (27), we need to continue to use an estimate for the total energy dissipated across the shock, based on the Mach number finder. For simplified test calculation with the code, we have also implemented an option where we assume a constant injection efficiency of the shock acceleration process, along with a constant spectral index and momentum cut-off parameter. Values for these parameters can then be chosen to represent the energetically most important types of shocks in the environment to be simulated. Such a simplified injection mechanism can then also be used to get an idea about the importance of the Mach-number dependence of the shock acceleration for different environments. # 2.4. Injection of cosmic rays by supernovae Strong shock waves associated with supernovae explosions are believed to be one of the most important cosmic ray injection mechanisms in the interstellar medium. However, similar to star formation itself, individual supernovae are far below our resolution limit in cosmological simulations where we need to represent whole galaxies, or more challenging still, sizable parts of the observable universe. We therefore resort to a subresolution treatment for star formation and its regulation by supernovae (Yepes et al. 1997; Springel & Hernquist 2003a). In this model, the interstellar medium is pictured as a multiphase medium composed of dense, cold clouds, embedded in a tenuous hot phase. The clouds form by thermal instability out of the diffuse medium, and are the sites of star formation. The massive stars of each formed stellar population are assumed to explode instantly, heating the hot phase, and evaporating some of the cold clouds. In this way, a tight self-regulation cycle for star formation in the ISM is established. In simulations where we include star formation, we employ the default parameters of Springel & Hernquist (2003a), for which stars can form above a threshold gas density $\rho_{\star} = 8.55 \times 10^{6} h^{-2} M_{\odot} kpc^{-3}$ , and 10% of the stellar mass is formed in massive stars that explode as supernovae. With these settings, the model reproduces the observed “Kennicutt Law” (Kennicutt 1989) of star formation in low-redshift disk galaxies. To model the generation of cosmic rays, we assume that a certain fraction $\zeta_{SN} \simeq 0.1-0.3$ of the supernova energy appears as a cosmic ray population (Aharonian et al. 2006; Kang & Jones 2006). The total rate of energy injection by supernovae for a given star formation rate $\dot{\rho}_{\star}$ depends on the IMF. Assuming a Salpeter IMF and that stars above a mass of $8 M_{\odot}$ explode as supernova with a canonical energy release of $10^{51}$ erg, we obtain roughly one supernova per $250 M_{\odot}$ of stellar mass formed, translating to an energy injection rate per unit volume of $\epsilon_{SN}\dot{\rho}_{\star}$ , with $\epsilon_{SN} = 4 \times 10^{48}$ erg $M_{\odot}^{-1}$ . We then model the CR energy injection per timestep of a gas particle as $$
\Delta \tilde {\varepsilon} _ {\mathrm{SN}} = \zeta_ {\mathrm{SN}} \epsilon_ {\mathrm{SN}} \dot {m} _ {\star} \Delta t, \tag {31}
$$ where $\dot{m}_{\star} = \dot{\rho}_{\star} / \rho$ is the particle's star formation rate per unit mass. Note that uncertainties in the IMF are not really important here as we have introduced a free parameter, $\zeta_{\mathrm{SN}}$ , to control the amount of energy that is fed into cosmic rays. For the slope of the injected cosmic ray power-law we assume a plausible value of $\alpha_{SN} = 2.4$ (Aharonian et al. 2004, 2006), and for the cut-off $q_{SN}$ , we can adopt the thermal momentum $q_{\mathrm{SN}} = \sqrt{kT_{\mathrm{SN}}/(m_{\mathrm{p}}c^{2})}$ for a fiducial supernova temperature characteristic for the involved shock acceleration. Our choice of $\alpha_{SN} = 2.4$ for the injection slope is motivated by the observed slope of $\sim2.75$ in the ISM, and the realization that momentum dependent diffusion in a turbulent magnetic field with a Kolmogorov-type spectrum on small scales should steepen the injected spectrum by $p^{-1/3}$ in equilibrium. Our results do not depend on the particular choice for $T_{SN}$ , provided $q_{SN} \ll 1$ . The change of the particle number density can then be computed with the mean kinetic energy $T_{\mathrm{CR}}(\alpha_{\mathrm{SN}}, q_{\mathrm{SN}})$ of the injected power law. Using Eqs. (4) and (11), this results in $$
\Delta \tilde {n} = m _ {\mathrm{p}} \frac {\zeta_ {\mathrm{SN}} \epsilon_ {\mathrm{SN}} \dot {m} _ {\star}}{T _ {\mathrm{CR}} (\alpha_ {\mathrm{SN}} , q _ {\mathrm{SN}})} \Delta t. \tag {32}
$$ We note that in the formalism of Springel & Hernquist (2003a), we need to reduce the supernovae energy injected into thermal feedback (and to an optional wind model if used) by the fraction $\zeta_{SN}$ that we assume powers cosmic ray acceleration. Also, similar to our treatment of shock injection, $\Delta\tilde{n}$ will be lowered in practice when we shift the actual injection point from $q_{SN}$ to $q_{inj}$ , as determined by Eq. (18). The resulting difference of the energies is fed directly to the thermal reservoir, contributing to the ordinary supernova feedback modelled by the simulation code. # 2.5. Coulomb cooling and catastrophic losses Charged particles moving through a plasma will gradually dissipate their kinetic energy and transfer it to the surrounding ions and electrons by Coulomb interactions. The rate of this energy loss depends both on the physical properties of the surrounding medium and on the detailed momentum spectrum of the cosmic ray population. The latter in particular complicates an accurate determination of the Coulomb loss rate.

As one of the most famous astrophysical objects in the Galaxy, the Crab Nebula has been observed at energies ranging from radio to ultrahigh-energy $\gamma$ -rays. It is powered by the energetic pulsar PSR J0534+2200 (or the Crab pulsar) and is located at a distance of 2 kpc (Manchester et al. 2005). For the Crab pulsar, it has a rotation period of $P = 33.4$ ms, a period derivative of $\dot{P} = 4.21 \times 10^{-13}$ s s $^{-1}$ , a characteristic age of 1260 yr, and a braking index of 2.509 (e.g., Lyne et al. 1993; Taylor et al. 1993). Besides, the record in Chinese chronicles shows that the Crab pulsar maybe has a typical age of 967 yr (Lundmark 1921). To perform the fit of the spectral energy distribution (SED) of the Crab Nebula, we acquired the multiband observational data as same as those shown in Zhu et al. (2021) and Peng et al. (2022), as follows: Baldwin (1971) and Macías-Pérez et al. (2010) for the radio band; Ney & Stein (1968), Grasdalen (1979) and Temim et al. (2006) for the infrared (IR) band; Veron-Cetty & Woltjer (1993) for the optical band; Hennessy et al. (1992) and Kuiper et al. (2001) for the X-ray and soft γ-ray bands; Aharonian et al. (2004, 2006), Albert et al. (2008), Abdo et al. (2010), Amenomori et al. (2019) and Cao et al. (2021) for the gigaelectronvolt and teraelectronvolt γ-ray bands. Meanwhile, in the fitting of the observations, a Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) method is employed to explore the parameter space (emcee; Foreman-Mackey et al. 2013, and references therein). In the calculations of the IC loss and spectrum, the background soft photon fields involved in the IC processes are as follows (e.g., Martín et al. 2012; Torres et al. 2014): the FIR (NIR) with temperature 70 (5000) K has an energy density of 0.5 (1.0) eV cm $^{-3}$ , and the 2.73 K CMB has a density of 0.25 eV cm $^{-3}$ . To model the dynamical radius of the nebula, the explosion energy and ejecta mass of the supernova are chosen to be $E_{sn} = 1 \times 10^{51}$ erg and $M_{ej} = 10 M_{\odot}$ , respectively. Moreover, for the interstellar medium density in the Crab Nebula, we simply assume a fiducial value of 1.0 cm $^{-3}$ (e.g., Tanaka & Takahara 2010; Martín et al. 2012). Note that, the current spin-down luminosity of the Crab pulsar is treated as a fitting parameter in our model because the spin-down luminosity strongly depends on the pulsar's moment of inertia (see Equation (2)), but it is poorly constrained. The properties of the turbulence in the PWNe are still unclear, so we consider the classical Kolmogorov- and Kraichnan-type turbulence in the nebula, respectively, which are respectively referred to as models KO and KR. According to the MCMC method, the best-fitted parameters for the Crab Nebula are thus can be obtained. The values of the parameters are listed in Table 1. As will be found below, the multiband photon spectra of the Crab Nebula are reproduced, the distributions of the energy and spatial diffusion coefficients predicted by both the Kolmogorov- and Kraichnan-type turbulent model are presented, and the comparisons of the modeling results in the models KO and KR with those obtained in the typical Bohm diffusion model are shown, as well as the effects of the break between the energy and inertial range of the injected waves on the particle diffusion are investigated. # 3.1. Kolmogorov-type Turbulence In Figure 1, the modeling results of the SED and the 1D probability distributions of the parameters obtained in the model KO are shown. It can be found that the multiband photon emission from the Crab Nebula can be naturally explained. As exhibited in the plot, the photons ranging from radio to the X-rays are produced by synchrotron radiation of the electrons, the gigaelectronvolt and teraelectronvolt $\gamma$ -rays are mainly generated from the SSC processes, and the IC scattering off the FIR and the CMB is the lesser contributors to the high-energy spectrum, which are consistent with the results of Tanaka & Takahara (2010) and Martín et al. (2012) in the Table 1 Best-fitting Values of the Parameters for the Crab Nebula <table><tr><td>Model</td><td> $\log_{10}L_{\text{sd}}$  (erg s $^{-1}$ )</td><td> $\eta_{\text{w}}$  (%)</td><td> $\alpha_1$ </td><td> $\alpha_2$ </td><td> $\log_{10}\gamma_{\text{b}}$ </td><td> $\log_{10}\gamma_{\text{max}}$ </td></tr><tr><td>KO</td><td> $38.587^{+0.004}_{-0.004}$ </td><td> $27.806^{+0.676}_{-0.695}$ </td><td> $1.509^{+0.010}_{-0.011}$ </td><td> $2.429^{+0.009}_{-0.009}$ </td><td> $5.964^{+0.041}_{-0.043}$ </td><td> $9.860^{+0.019}_{-0.019}$ </td></tr><tr><td>KR</td><td> $38.880^{+0.008}_{-0.009}$ </td><td> $9.578^{+0.379}_{-0.375}$ </td><td> $1.755^{+0.023}_{-0.023}$ </td><td> $2.348^{+0.007}_{-0.006}$ </td><td> $4.692^{+0.059}_{-0.062}$ </td><td> $9.805^{+0.021}_{-0.020}$ </td></tr></table> framework of the non-diffusion and diffusion-loss models, respectively. From the modeling results, the current spin-down luminosity of the Crab pulsar is estimated to be $3.86 \times 10^{38}$ erg s $^{-1}$ , just slightly smaller than the value of $4.46 \times 10^{38}$ erg s $^{-1}$ derived from the observations with a standard pulsar's moment of inertia of $10^{45}$ g cm $^{2}$ . On the other hand, our model allows a fraction of 27.8% spin-down energy to be converted into turbulence energy. Then, according to Equation (8), we obtain the current magnetic field of the Crab Nebula is 109 $\mu$ G, very close to the result of Cao et al. (2021) estimated by the observations of Large High Altitude Air Shower Observatory. However, for the break energy of the injected electrons, it is adopted to be about 0.5 TeV in our model, larger than the value of 0.1–0.3 TeV given by the previous works in the literature (e.g., Zhang et al. 2008; Tanaka & Takahara 2010; Martín et al. 2012; Zhu et al. 2021), due to different treatments of the spatial diffusion and acceleration of the particles within the nebula. According to Equations (11) and (15), the energy and spatial diffusion coefficients of the particles in our model can be approximated as $$
D _ {\mathrm{EE}} \propto \gamma k _ {\mathrm{res}} w (k _ {\mathrm{res}}), \tag {18}
$$ and $$
D _ {\mathrm{RR}} \propto \frac {\gamma}{k _ {\mathrm{res}} w (k _ {\mathrm{res}})}, \tag {19}
$$ indicating both the energy and spatial diffusion coefficients are mainly determined by the distribution of the turbulence waves. These simplifying expressions are mainly used to analyze the relationship between the energy and the space diffusion coefficient with the turbulent spectrum for convenience. In the modeling of the photon emission from the Crab Nebula, the exact expressions shown in Equations (11) and (15) are employed. Note that, in our calculations, the relation between the spatial and energy diffusion coefficient is simplified and described as $D_{RR}D_{EE} = \nu_{A}^{2}\gamma^{2}/9$ , which is derived from the approximation of the QLT and would probably be more suitable for the weak turbulence (Michałek & Ostrowsky 1996; Dempers & Engelbrecht 2020). In the case of the strong turbulence, the relationship may be more complex and would have some divergence from the QLT ones. In practice, according to the Monte Carlo simulations, Michałek & Ostrowsky (1996) noted that the divergence of the relationship between the spatial and energy diffusion coefficient seems to not be very large. Therefore, we utilize the approximation derived from the QLT to describe the relationship between spatial and energy diffusion coefficients in this paper for simplicity. On the other hand, from Equations (7) and (12) with the Kolmogorov-type turbulence considered, it can be obtained that $$
\tau_ {\mathrm{cas}} \propto k ^ {- 3 / 2} [ w (k) ] ^ {- 1 / 2}, \tag {20}
$$ and $$
\tau_ {\mathrm{dam}} = 1 / \Gamma_ {\mathrm{k}} \propto \frac {k}{n (\gamma_ {\mathrm{res}})}, \tag {21}
$$ revealing the damping process for the turbulence waves is related to the number density of the electrons, which yields the distribution of the turbulence waves, as well as the energy cascade process, would be affected by the distribution of the electrons. It can thus be found that in the wave–particle systems, the distributions of turbulence waves and electrons are determined by the interactions of the turbulence waves and electrons with each other. Therefore, four scenarios are introduced to further illustrate the effects of the energy cascade and damping processes, as well as the acceleration and spatial diffusion processes, on the turbulent and electron spectra, respectively, as follows: (1) NO-DAM: for the turbulence waves, the energy cascade process is taken into account while the damping process is not included. (2) NO-CAS: for the turbulence waves, the damping process is considered but the energy cascade process is neglected.

