The rate of the destruction of the adiabatic invariant can be diffusive with $D_{\alpha_{eq}\alpha_{eq}} \propto \varepsilon^{2}$ (see reviews by D. L. Vainchtein et al., 2005; Zelenyi et al., 2013, and references therein) or drift-like with $\langle \Delta I_{\parallel} \rangle \propto \varepsilon$ (Ukhorskiy, Sitnov, et al., 2011; Vainshtein et al., 1999). This brief overview of $I_{\perp}$ and $I_{\parallel}$ destruction illustrates the basic idea that there are two overlapping and coexisting theoretical approaches describing diffusive and non-diffusive (often refer as nonlinear) destruction of these invariants. Making a direct analogy with the invariant of the azimuthal motion, we will consider two branches of models of this invariant destruction—diffusion on weak random perturbations and non-diffusive destruction due to intense perturbations. After the gyro-averaging and bounce-averaging, the electron dynamics is reduced to a drift across magnetic field lines with a characteristic velocity $v_{D} \sim H / \Omega_{ce} R_{c} m_{e}$ . Due to the energy conservation, this drift occurs along lines of constant $\Omega_{ce}$ , which, in turn, depends on equatorial coordinates ( $R \cos \phi, R \sin \phi$ ). In azimuthally symmetric (e.g., dipole) fields, $\Omega_{ce}$ is a function of the radial distance, $R$ , only and therefore the drift preserves $R$ and moves electrons around the planet (for a detailed analysis of situations when azimuthal symmetry is violated and no azimuthal averaging can be done, see Lejosne & Albert, 2023; Lejosne et al., 2023; Osmane et al., 2023). Figure 1e illustrates this type of periodic motion. The corresponding adiabatic invariant $I_{\phi} = (2\pi)^{-1} \oint P_{\phi} R d\phi \approx e RA_0 / c$ is the magnetic flux across surface stretched on an azimuthal electron trajectory (taking into account that velocity of this drift is much smaller than $eA_0 / c$ ). Therefore, the conservation of $I_{\phi}$ guarantees that electrons do not move radially. Destruction of this invariant is associated with forces acting on time-scales of the electron azimuthal drift (see discussion in Ukhorskiy & Sitnov, 2013). Figure 1f shows these time-scales for electrons of different energies and at different radial distances. Theoretical models of the $I_{\phi}$ destruction mostly incorporate a linear response of electron dynamics to ultra-low-frequency (ULF) waves. For systems with broad-band ULF waves, the linear response results in the diffusion of $I_{\phi}$ , and thus in the radial diffusion $D_{RR} \sim D_{I_{\phi}I_{\phi}}$ (see original studies of such ULF-driven radial diffusion in Birmingham, 1969; Fälthammar, 1965; Parker, 1960; Schulz & Eviatar, 1969; Tverskoy, 1969). There are multiple theoretical aspects of such radial diffusion, and the corresponding models are still being developed to address new spacecraft observations (see review Lejosne, 2019; Lejosne & Kollmann, 2020, and references therein). Because a quite significant fraction of ULF waves are observed with a narrow-band spectrum, their impact on electron flux dynamics may be rather coherent (see observations in Claudepierre et al., 2013; Mann et al., 2013; Teramoto et al., 2019; Zhao et al., 2021; Q. G. Zong et al., 2009). Transition from such coherent impact to stochastic one (i.e., known to cause the radial diffusion) requires additional stochasticization factors, that are widely discussed in the literature (see, e.g., Elkington et al., 2003; Ukhorskiy et al., 2005). In the absence of external stochasticization, linear models of the electron resonant interaction with narrow-band (quasi-monochromatic) ULF waves reproduce the observed electron flux modulation rather well (see, e.g., discussion in Degeling et al., 2014; Hao et al., 2017; C. Wang et al., 2018). However, amplitudes of quasi-monochromatic waves may be sufficiently large to drive the nonlinear response of electron flux dynamics and the associated rapid $I_{\phi}$ destruction. Until recently, there were no models describing nonlinear destruction of $I_{\phi}$ , similar to models for the destruction of $I_{\perp}$ and $I_{\parallel}$ . In this commentary paper, we overview the rapidly developing theoretical approach that incorporate effects of nonlinear electron interactions with ULF waves into the arsenal of method used to describe radiation belts (see also review by Zhou et al., 2025). We start with main observational evidences suggesting a possibility of such nonlinear interactions (Section 2). Then we provide an overview of existing models of nonlinear electron interactions with ULF waves (Section 3). Finally, we discuss the importance of these interactions for the radiation belts dynamics (Section 4). # 2. Overview of Quasi-Monochromatic ULF Observations To apply the quasi-linear theory describing wave particle resonant interactions, one must assume that each individual interaction is short (Drummond & Pines, 1962; Vedenov et al., 1962). This criterion can be written as an inequality for width of the resonance in velocity space, $\Delta v_{R}$ , and the separation between resonances for two wave harmonics within the spectrum, $\delta v$ : $\delta v > \Delta v_{R}$ (the so-called Chirikov criterion; see Chirikov, 1987; Lichtenberg & Lieberman, 1983; Sagdeev et al., 1988). $\delta v_{R} \approx v_{R}(\omega + \Delta \omega) - v_{R}(\omega)$ is determined by the width of the frequency spectrum, $\Delta \omega$ , of ULF waves, whereas the resonance width is determined by the wave intensity, $\Delta v_{R} \propto \langle B_{w}^{2} \rangle^{1/4}$ . For the resonant interaction of ULF waves and electrons, the resonance condition is $m\omega_{D} = \omega$ , where $m$ is the azimuthal wave number, $\omega$ is ULF wave frequency, and $\omega_{D} = v_{D}/R$ is the azimuthal drift frequency ( $R = R_{E}L$ and for the dipole field $R = 3R_{c}$ ). Therefore, the resonant electron velocity is $v_{R} = R\omega/m$ , $\delta v_{R} = R\Delta \omega/m$ , and $$
\Delta v _ {R} \approx v _ {D} \Bigg (\mathfrak {m} \frac {\left<   E _ {w} ^ {2} \right> ^ {1 / 2}}{B _ {0}} \frac {c}{\omega R} \Bigg) ^ {1 / 2}
$$ where $\left\langle E_{w}^{2}\right\rangle^{1/2}$ is the averaged ULF wave amplitude (see Artemyev et al., 2024). Taking into account that $v_{D} = \omega_{D}R = \omega R/m$ , and $\left\langle E_{w}^{2}\right\rangle^{1/2} = (\omega/kc)\left\langle B_{w}^{2}\right\rangle^{1/2}$ where k = R/m is the ULF wave number, the condition $\delta v_{R} > \Delta v_{R}$ takes the form $$
\frac {\Delta \omega}{\omega} > \left\langle \frac {B _ {w} ^ {2}}{B _ {0} ^ {2}} \right\rangle^ {1 / 4}
$$ For typical ULF waves, $\left\langle B_w^2 /B_0^2\right\rangle^{1 / 2}\sim 10^{-2}$ , and the width of the wave spectrum should be wider than $10\%$ of the mean frequency to make the quasi-linear theory applicable. ULF waves are often observed in a form of broadband magnetic and electric field fluctuations (Hartinger et al., 2023; Murphy et al., 2018; Sandhu et al., 2018; Takahashi et al., 2015), and spectrum of such fluctuations is the main input parameter for modeling of radial diffusion. However, the applicability condition for the quasi-linear theory can be violated for quasi-monochromatic (narrow-band) intense ULF waves (see discussion of observations of such narrow-band waves in Murphy et al., 2020). In the Earth's magnetosphere, several types of narrow-band ULF waves may exist with properties varying according to the global magnetosphere configuration (e.g., radial Alfven speed profile, magnetopause location) and driving condition (see examples of such intense and quasi-monochromatic waves in, e.g., Claudepierre et al., 2013; Dai et al., 2015; Rubtsov et al., 2021; Yamamoto et al., 2024). These include the toroidal mode with predominant radial electric/azimuthal magnetic field disturbance and poloidal mode with predominant azimuthal electric/radial and compressional magnetic field disturbance. Both wave modes have field-aligned structure consistent with standing waves (i.e., nodes and anti-nodes, or local maxima/minima in electric/magnetic field that depend on the specific harmonic examined). A recent review of 3D electromagnetic field configuration of ULF waves can be found in Elsden et al. (2022). Example published observations of these two wave modes taken near the magnetic equatorial plane are shown in Figures 1g and 1h. For these two events $\left\langle B_w^2 /B_0^2\right\rangle^{1 / 4}$ reaches 0.15-0.2, whereas $\Delta \omega /\omega$ is well below 0.1 (see more examples in Zhou et al., 2025). Many other wave modes can also have narrow bandwidth in some circumstances (including compressional waves that carry energy across field lines) and in realistic magnetic field geometries all these wave modes can resonantly mode convert and have mixed polarization (e.g., Wright et al., 2024, and references therein). Whether or not the waves will interact with electrons resonantly depends on their phase speeds, polarization, and other parameters.

Vertical transport of neutral species is calculated between the intervals, except for O( $^{1}$ D) and N( $^{2}$ D), due to the fact that their chemical lifetimes are much shorter than the transport time scale. The one-dimensional approach of SIC is fully sufficient for the present study of local heating effects, since an air parcel cannot be heated for a time interval longer than its traversal of the heater beam. The D-region mean free path is sufficiently short so as to justify the assumption that ion, electron and neutral temperatures are equal during undisturbed conditions, i.e. whenever the heater is off. For the present study, however, the electron temperature $T_{e}$ must be separated from the ion/neutral temperature T when calculating the reaction rates of electron attachment and ion-electron recombination, since these will be affected by the heating. The reaction rate coefficients affected by this separation are listed in Table 1. We assume that the use of these same reaction rate coefficients is justifiable since the electron temperature will not deviate from the neutral temperature by more than one order of magnitude (see Rees, 1989). During the subsequent SIC runs, the electron temperature was either set equal to the neutral temperature (henceforth referred to as “background runs”) or calculated by the electron heating model described in Sect. 3.1 (“heating runs”). Assuming overall neutrality, we can calculate the electron density from the model results as $$
N _ {\mathrm{e}} = N _ {\mathrm{p}} - N _ {\mathrm{n}}, \tag {1}
$$ where $N_{p}$ and $N_{n}$ are the total concentrations of positive and negative ions, respectively. # 3 Estimating the effects of RF heating # 3.1 The electron heating model The electron temperature is calculated by a parallel-layer model described by Kero et al. (2000). The principle follows Belova et al. (1995) and is shown schematically in Fig. 1. For each model layer i, the dissipation $Q_{i}$ of radio wave energy by electron heating is calculated from the imaginary part of the refractive index n according to $$
